ENV001  Many conda transactions
  Why: Repeated environment changes make the original dependency choices harder to review and reproduce.
  Fix: Export the prefix with `conda env export --prefix PATH --from-history`, review the requested dependencies, and create a fresh environment from that specification.
  Enabled profiles: container-development (info), local (info)
  Docs:
    https://docs.conda.io/projects/conda/en/stable/user-guide/tasks/manage-environments.html
  Exceptions:
    The revision threshold uses conda's zero-based last transaction index; the displayed transaction count is revision + 1.
    History does not prove drift or cover pip installs or file edits. A from-history export is not a resolved lock.

ENV002  Project has no lockfile
  Why: A resolved lock records dependency choices that a manifest alone leaves open.
  Fix: In the associated project, run `pixi lock`, `conda-lock lock`, or the locking command for its package manager.
  Enabled profiles: local (info)
  Docs:
    https://pixi.sh/latest/workspace/lockfile/
    https://conda.github.io/conda-lock/
  Exceptions:
    Only the explicitly associated project root is checked for recognized lockfiles.
    Presence does not establish installation synchronization; unsupported lock formats receive a presence check only.

ENV003  Python packages installed outside conda
  Why: Packages outside conda's file ownership are not managed by conda's dependency solver.
  Fix: Record intentional pip dependencies in the project specification; install conda dependencies first and pip dependencies last when recreating the environment.
  Enabled profiles: local (info)
  Docs:
    https://docs.conda.io/projects/conda/en/stable/user-guide/tasks/manage-environments.html#using-pip-in-an-environment
  Exceptions:
    Unowned distribution metadata does not identify the installer; manual and editable installs are possible.
    Incomplete conda ownership metadata cannot establish that a distribution is unowned.

ENV004  Python package conflicts with conda metadata
  Why: Different versions of one distribution can leave conda's recorded state inconsistent with Python package metadata.
  Fix: Recreate the environment with one owner for the conflicting package; avoid installing a different pip version over a conda-managed distribution.
  Enabled profiles: local (info)
  Docs:
    https://docs.conda.io/projects/conda/en/stable/user-guide/tasks/manage-environments.html#using-pip-in-an-environment
  Exceptions:
    A metadata version conflict does not prove Python import precedence or identify the installer; Python versions use PEP 440 equivalence.
    Conda package names and PyPI distribution names are not universally interchangeable; bundles can use different package and distribution version conventions.

ENV005  Extra packages in the tooling environment
  Why: Application dependencies in the installer environment couple its maintenance to project dependencies.
  Fix: Create a separate project environment with `conda create --name PROJECT PACKAGE...` and keep the installer environment focused on its tooling.
  Enabled profiles: local (info)
  Docs:
    https://docs.conda.io/projects/conda/en/stable/user-guide/tasks/manage-environments.html
  Exceptions:
    A conda/mamba-containing prefix is a tooling candidate, not proof of the installation's base prefix or application use.
    Packages outside the tools' declared dependency closure may be intentional tooling extensions.

ENV006  Duplicate conda package sets
  Why: Repeated conda package sets may duplicate environment maintenance and disk usage.
  Fix: Review the listed prefixes and remove an unused one with `conda env remove --prefix PATH` only after checking its project references.
  Enabled profiles: local (info)
  Docs:
    https://docs.conda.io/projects/conda/en/stable/user-guide/tasks/manage-environments.html#removing-an-environment
  Exceptions:
    Only prefixes in the requested discovery scope are compared.
    Matching conda identities do not establish matching pip packages, files, configuration or runtime behavior, or predict disk savings.

ENV007  Defaults and conda-forge mixed
  Why: Mixing defaults and conda-forge can combine packages built against different dependency assumptions.
  Fix: Choose one channel family, set strict channel priority, and recreate the environment; for conda-forge use `conda create --override-channels -c conda-forge --strict-channel-priority --name PROJECT PACKAGE...`.
  Enabled profiles: local (warning)
  Docs:
    https://conda-forge.org/docs/user/tipsandtricks/
    https://docs.conda.io/projects/conda/en/stable/user-guide/tasks/manage-channels.html
  Exceptions:
    Intentional mixtures are possible; this check does not prove an ABI failure.
    Only canonical official channel identities are classified; custom aliases and defaults multichannel expansion are unsupported.

ENV008  Host package cache content
  Why: Cached downloads and extracted packages consume space and can be shared by several environments.
  Fix: Review `conda clean --tarballs --dry-run` and `conda clean --packages --dry-run` before cleaning unused cache content; preserve caches required by symlink-installed environments.
  Enabled profiles: local (info)
  Docs:
    https://docs.conda.io/projects/conda/en/stable/commands/clean.html
  Exceptions:
    Cache roots come from installed records, not complete conda configuration precedence; shared roots are inspected once per run and archives and extracted entries can represent the same package.
    Only archive file sizes are measured; extracted directories are identified by canonical markers without traversing their payload. Sizes are not unique disk blocks or reclaimable space. Do not use --force-pkgs-dirs for symlink-installed environments.

ENV009  Python version is past upstream end-of-life
  Why: Upstream CPython no longer issues fixes for an end-of-life release series.
  Fix: Create and test a replacement environment with a supported Python release, for example `conda create --name PROJECT python=3.13 PACKAGE...`.
  Enabled profiles: local (warning)
  Docs:
    https://devguide.python.org/versions/
  Exceptions:
    The upstream CPython support table is dated 2026-10-26 and evaluated against the report's evaluation date.
    Vendor-maintained interpreters may have a different support policy; EOL is not a vulnerability finding.

ENV010  Installed conda metadata differs from pixi.lock
  Why: Installed conda package metadata differs from the Pixi lock selection used for this prefix.
  Fix: Run `pixi install --environment ENVIRONMENT` in the project that owns the prefix, then repeat this check.
  Enabled profiles: local (warning)
  Docs:
    https://pixi.sh/latest/workspace/lockfile/
  Exceptions:
    The Pixi marker supplies the environment and platform; missing selection or incomplete records cannot establish missing packages.
    Only conda metadata identities and available artifact hashes are compared, not PyPI, installed files, Pixi's installed-lock hash or the manifest.

ENV011  pixi.lock is stale against its manifest
  Why: A stale manifest/lock selection can omit requested dependency or platform changes.
  Fix: Run `pixi lock` in the project to refresh its resolved dependency selections.
  Enabled profiles: local (warning)
  Docs:
    https://pixi.sh/latest/workspace/lockfile/
  Exceptions:
    Timestamps, package counts and satisfying individual conda specs do not establish manifest/lock freshness.
  Unavailable: go-rattler does not provide authoritative Pixi manifest/lock synchronization; MatchSpec matching covers only part of that contract.

ENV012  Requested packages have no version constraint
  Why: An unconstrained request can select a different version when the environment is recreated.
  Fix: Add suitable version constraints to the project specification and resolve a lock with `pixi lock` or `conda-lock lock`.
  Enabled profiles: local (info)
  Docs:
    https://docs.conda.io/projects/conda/en/stable/user-guide/concepts/pkg-specs.html
  Exceptions:
    Missing requested-spec metadata is not an unconstrained request.
    A version range is a constraint even when it is not an exact version/build pin; this check does not prove installed drift.
    If a record has several requested specs, any unconstrained request is reported; this check does not test whether the installed version satisfies them.

ENV013  CA packages below eMudhra remediation minimum
  Why: Applications using an environment's CA packages need the distribution's recommended updates following the October 2026 eMudhra intermediate-certificate compromise.
  Fix: In virtualenvs, update certifi to >=2026.10.18 with the environment's package manager. In conda prefixes, update certifi to >=2026.10.19 and ca-certificates to >=2026.10.19 on conda-forge or >=2026.10.20 on Anaconda main; update image builds and project locks as appropriate. Keep certificate verification enabled.
  Enabled profiles: container (warning), container-development (warning), container-distroless (warning), container-runtime (warning), local (warning)
  Docs:
    https://conda.org/blog/2026-10-21-emudhra-security-updates/
    https://github.com/conda-forge/conda-forge.github.io/issues/2967
    https://github.com/certifi/python-certifi/security/advisories/GHSA-6q48-6c7w-9gw2
  Exceptions:
    Minimums were verified on 2026-10-26 for the official conda-forge and Anaconda main channels; custom or missing channel identities are unknown.
    certifi 2026.10.18 already fixes the incident but falls below the conda distributions' recommended minimum; it meets the upstream virtualenv minimum. Older metadata is not proof that an affected certificate remains or is trusted.
    Only conda package metadata and local virtualenv certifi distribution metadata are checked, not inherited system packages, pip-vendored copies, PEM contents, custom CA files or the operating system trust store.
    Python/package pins or retired platforms may prevent updates; use a supported environment or distribution rather than disabling TLS verification.

IMG001  Package archives shipped in image
  Why: Cached downloads add content to the image after installation has finished.
  Fix: Add `conda clean --tarballs -y` to the same `RUN` step that installs packages, or keep downloads in a BuildKit cache mount.
  Enabled profiles: container (info), container-development (info), container-distroless (warning), container-runtime (warning)
  Docs:
    https://docs.conda.io/projects/conda/en/stable/commands/clean.html
    https://docs.docker.com/build/cache/optimize/
  Exceptions:
    Only canonical package archive filenames inside observed package caches count; archive bytes are not format-verified.
    Runtime package installation may intentionally use archives. File payload bytes are not predicted image-size savings.

IMG002  Extracted package cache shipped in image
  Why: Extracted caches can leave installation content in the image that its application does not need.
  Fix: Review `conda clean --packages --dry-run` during installation, or copy a tested application prefix into a compatible runtime stage. Preserve observed cache dependencies.
  Enabled profiles: container (info), container-development (info), container-distroless (warning), container-runtime (warning)
  Docs:
    https://docs.conda.io/projects/conda/en/stable/commands/clean.html
    https://docs.docker.com/build/building/multi-stage/
  Exceptions:
    Symlinks can require cache paths; unlinking one ordinary hardlink does not erase another hardlink's content.
    Image hardlink identity can be unresolved. File payload totals are not unique or reclaimable bytes, and --packages removes only unused cache entries.

IMG003  Installer shipped alongside application environment
  Why: A separate installer prefix can add build tooling to an image whose application uses another prefix.
  Fix: Copy the tested application prefix into a compatible runtime stage and validate startup, activation, runtime libraries and relocation.
  Enabled profiles: container (info), container-development (info), container-distroless (info), container-runtime (info)
  Docs:
    https://docs.docker.com/build/building/multi-stage/
  Exceptions:
    Application-in-base images are valid; direct startup using tooling or its prefix passes this check.
    Nested envs/ paths identify application candidates, not exclusive runtime use. Shell wrappers are not interpreted.

IMG004  Cache hidden by later layers still ships
  Why: Deleting or replacing files in a later layer does not remove their earlier layer content from the image.
  Fix: Install and clean caches in the same `RUN` step, use `RUN --mount=type=cache` for downloads, or copy the tested prefix into a runtime stage.
  Enabled profiles: container-distroless (warning), container-runtime (warning)
  Docs:
    https://docs.docker.com/build/building/multi-stage/
    https://docs.docker.com/build/cache/optimize/
  Exceptions:
    Compressed descriptor bytes describe whole layers, never per-file reclaimable savings; displayed source layers are numbered from one.
    Collection does not identify deletion layers or Dockerfile instructions. Surviving cache hardlinks must not be classified as runtime-unused content.

IMG005  Package manager executables shipped in image
  Why: Package-manager executables add installation tooling and attack surface to an application image.
  Fix: Install dependencies in a builder stage and copy tested runtime contents into a compatible final image; preserve package databases, metadata and required libraries.
  Enabled profiles: container (info), container-development (info), container-distroless (error), container-runtime (warning)
  Docs:
    https://docs.docker.com/build/building/multi-stage/
    https://github.com/GoogleContainerTools/distroless
    https://github.com/GoogleContainerTools/distroless/blob/main/PACKAGE_METADATA.md
  Exceptions:
    Package databases, metadata, libraries, documentation and matching basenames alone are not command tooling.
    Only recognized command locations are inspected; executable presence does not prove installation will succeed.
    Intentional package-management and development workloads may retain these tools.

IMG006  Shell present under shell-free image policy
  Why: A shell conflicts with the shell-free policy selected by the distroless profile or this explicit rule.
  Fix: Use exec-form `CMD` with a shell-free compatible base. For intentional shells, select `--profile container-runtime` and remove any explicit IMG006 enablement, or record a reviewed suppression.
  Enabled profiles: container-distroless (error)
  Docs:
    https://github.com/GoogleContainerTools/distroless
  Exceptions:
    Intentional debug images and shell-dependent startup require an explicit reviewed exception.
    Recognized command locations do not establish absence at custom installation paths.

